      "id": "aff08f62444e3f170a48459c3224a748a1fae73ebba313e85565873ca4655ae7",
      "title": "The stories that explain a historic election in America",
      "url": "https://www.poynter.org/business-work/2026/usa-today-co-ai-use-advertising-video",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-16T16:53:40.266Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.poynter.org/business-work/2026/usa-today-co-ai-use-advertising-video/",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "USA Today Co. blames AI for its revenue woes. It also sees AI as a solution. - Poynter",
      "pageDescription": "Traffic and digital revenue have declined thanks to changes in search behavior, CEO Mike Reed said",
      "evidenceText": "USA Today Co. blames AI for its revenue woes. It also sees AI as a solution. - Poynter USA Today Co. blames AI for its revenue woes. It also sees AI as a solution. - Poynter DONATE What do you want to search? Submit Sign In | Membership News Training Ethics Fact-Checking Media Literacy AI Events Future of Public Media Newmark Ethics Center IFCN MediaWise PolitiFact Donate About Us Press Freedom Watch The Poynter 50 Podcasts Artificial Intelligence Business & Work Reporting & Editing Fact-Checking Ethics & Trust Local News Journalism, Media & Communication Training Poynter Membership Categories Consulting & Coaching For ACES Members For Local News Funders All Categories Broadcast & Visual Journalism Business & Work Ethics Fact-Checking & Media Literacy Leadership Reporting & Editing Writing Poynter ACES Introductory Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Intermediate Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Advanced Certificate in Editing Workshops and Coaching Tools for Funders Ethics Ethics Training Ethics Articles AI Ethics Public Editor Project Get Ethics Advice Fact-Checking Articles IFCN IFCN Grants International Fact-Checking Day Teen Creator Network PolitiFact MediaWise Media Literacy Resources AI Literacy Ambassadors Teen Creator Network Research & News International en Español MediaWise Training All Events The 2026 Poynter Journalism Prizes Business & Work Sections of a USA Today newspapers rest together, Monday, Aug. 5, 2019, in Norwood, Mass. (AP Photo/Steven Senne) By: Angela Fu November 5, 2026 USA Today Co. — the largest newspaper chain in the country — reported a lackluster third-quarter performance Thursday, including a net loss, declining revenue and setbacks in the company’s goals. The chain, which includes titles like The Arizona Republic and The Indianapolis Star, ended the quarter with a net loss of $4.6 million. While that was a noticeable improvement on the $39.2 million loss the company suffered during the same period last year, it meant that after two consecutive profitable quarters, USA Today Co. was once again in the red. Revenue was also down, falling 7.4% year-over-year to $519.2 million. The issue was artificial intelligence. Changes in search behavior, along with “some inflationary pressures,” hurt digital revenue, CEO Mike Reed said on an earnings call. Like many media outlets, USA Today Co. lost traffic as news consumers read AI-generated summaries served up by Google and other platforms and failed to click through. Though Reed stressed that declines were concentrated in a small part of digital advertising revenue — one that makes up less than 5% of the company’s total business — he acknowledged later in the call that the changes in search had had an “outsized impact” on USA Today Co.’s financial statements for the past couple of quarters. That impact has led USA Today Co. to revise its expectations. The company now predicts a steeper decline in same-store revenue next quarter and full-year declines in adjusted EBITDA and free cash flow. Instead of hitting its goal of 50% of total revenue from digital this year, USA Today Co. hopes to reach that milestone next year. “While these pressures have delayed our return to overall revenue growth, we believe we are building a foundation for improved performance,” chief financial officer Trisha Gosser said. To build that foundation, USA Today Co. is cutting costs and leaning on AI. One of those initiatives is its controversial partnership with technology company Palantir, which USA Today Co. first announced in August. That deal has allowed the company to use AI-powered tools from Palantir to analyze data from customers and readers and identify ways to monetize them. Though unions at dozens of USA Today Co. newspapers have decried the partnership, Reed said the first “products” are now live and starting to scale. USA Today Co. is also using AI to expand its video offerings, allowing it to grow video advertising, said Reed. The company aims to make all of its content available in video as well as text. It has also started creating branded advertising content on its platform that is AI-discoverable. “Because there’s so much crap and untrusted content out there on the AI platforms, it’s a real opportunity for us to partner with brands to create trusted messaging and to make that discoverable on AI platforms,” Reed said. Before the end of the year, USA Today Co. plans to announce “at least one, possibly two” more content licensing deals with AI companies — another source of revenue, Reed said. The company is also cutting costs. USA Today Co. executives revealed that the company had made $100 million in cuts in late September, including layoffs. The scope of those layoffs is still unclear, though many sports journalists were affected. The audience team was also reorganized. USA Today Co. will continue to make cuts in the coming year, Gosser said in response to an investor’s question. “We’ve already identified opportunities where we can lower our vendor spend through things like consolidating systems or our technology platforms next year,” Gosser said. “We’ll continue to optimize our outsourcing agreements. We’ll continue to optimize and streamline our print operations as that business evolves.” This kind of journalism doesn’t fund itself. Poynter helps you understand what’s happening in the media industry and how it shapes the news you read every day. Donations power our nonprofit newsroom and keep stories like these free for everyone. DONATE Tags: AI , Artificial Intelligence , earning report , Earnings reports , Gannett , USA Today Co. Angela Fu Angela Fu is a reporter for Poynter. She can be reached at afu@poynter.org, on Signal at angelafu.74, on Bluesky @angelanfu.bsky.social and on Twitter @angelanfu. Angela Fu Top business leaders at Poynter’s Tampa Bay Times to depart at end of 2026 CEO and Chairman Conan Gallaty and President and COO Bala Sundaramoorthy leave as part of a restructuring November 16, 2026 Jennifer Orsi Opinion | A wild weekend of travel delays, explanations and excuses After a nationwide flight shutdown stranded travelers, Trump stayed publicly silent for nearly two days November 16, 2026 Tom Jones How Ohio journalists exposed dangerous dog laws — and helped change them At the heart of the award-winning investigation was a commitment to earning the trust of people who had survived devastating dog attacks. November 16, 2026 Amaris Castillo After standing up for his ‘60 Minutes’ colleagues, Scott Pelley wants to explore the ‘triumph of truth’ ‘When autocrats around the world and through history attempt to take away all of the people's rights, they always start with the press’ November 15, 2026 Angela Fu Here’s what a Democratic-controlled Congress could do to protect press freedom Congressional investigations could put the administration’s attacks on the press under scrutiny, while bipartisan legislation offers a path to reform November 13, 2026 Angela Fu Back to News Comments Cancel reply You must be logged in to post a comment. This site uses Akismet to reduce spam. Learn how your comment data is processed. Become a Poynter Member Don't miss out on a year of learning. JOIN TODAY Already a Member? Log in Start your day informed and inspired. Subscribe Get the Poynter newsletter that's right for you. Media Jobs News Editor Director of Advertising DIGITAL SALES STRATEGIST BROADCAST JOURNALIST: ARTS & CULTURE Deputy Managing Editor- News Director of Technology Chair and Professor of the Practice Publisher and Chief Executive Officer - Connecticut Mirror / CT Mirror News Editor, The Stranger - Seattle, WA (98122) Group Editor - Columbia, MD (21045) Search Media Jobs Advertise a job &times; This kind of journalism doesn’t fund itself. Poynter helps you understand what’s happening in the media industry and how it shapes the news you read every day. Donations power our nonprofit newsroom and keep stories like these free for everyone. DONATE",
