eattle Seahawks AFC East Buffalo Bills Miami Dolphins New England Patriots New York Jets AFC North Baltimore Ravens Cincinnati Bengals Cleveland Browns Pittsburgh Steelers AFC South Houston Texans Indianapolis Colts Jacksonville Jaguars Tennessee Titans AFC West Denver Broncos Kansas City Chiefs Las Vegas Raiders Los Angeles Chargers NFC East Dallas Cowboys New York Giants Philadelphia Eagles Washington Commanders NFC North Chicago Bears Detroit Lions Green Bay Packers Minnesota Vikings NFC South Atlanta Falcons Carolina Panthers New Orleans Saints Tampa Bay Buccaneers NFC West Arizona Cardinals Los Angeles Rams San Francisco 49ers Seattle Seahawks Rumor Mill Peacock PFT Shop Dianna Russini gets out the word that she’s coming back to work next week By Mike Florio , By Mike Florio     Published November 4, 2026 04:21 PM Facebook Twitter Email The halfway point of the 2026 NFL season arrives this weekend. Next week, Dianna Russini apparently will return to sports media. Per a source with knowledge of the situation, Russini has begun getting the word out to people in the league that she’ll be returning to work “next week.” It’s unclear where she’ll be working, or what she’ll be doing. She resigned from The Athletic in April, one week after photos were published by the New York Post of Russini and Patriots coach Mike Vrabel at a resort in Arizona. In both her comments to the Post and her resignation letter to The Athletic, she denied that there was any type of impropriety. The Athletic conducted a review of her work. Its findings were released in early September. “Russini’s relationship with Vrabel was in clear violation of The Athletic ’s standards,” wrote Mike Semel of The Athletic , who conducted the investigation. “Regardless of whether the relationship was romantic, it was a breach and should have been disclosed.” Beyond the relationship was the manner in which she handled the emergence of the controversy. \"[W]hen first confronted with the photos by The New York Post ,” Semel wrote, “Russini waited two days before telling her editors and managers. She then told a story to explain the photos that did not align with facts, raising issues of trust.” Along the way, a strange story emerged regarding Russini being pulled over for texting while driving — made stranger by her decision to tell the story publicly and embellish the notion that she FaceTimed a head coach for help in getting out of the ticket. So if she’ll be reporting again, she’ll need to restore her own credibility. Especially given the unique set of facts that resulted in her departure from The Athletic . Read Now: Quarterback of the Future \" href=\"https://www.nbcsports.com/nfl/profootballtalk/quarterback-of-the-future\" > Read Now: Quarterback of the Future What happens when two modern-day college football players end up in 1960? They can either find real jobs, or they can try out for the local pro team. WATCH: PFT Live with Mike Florio \" href=\"https://www.nbcsports.com/nfl/profootballtalk/pft-live?utm_tags=nbcs1173\" target=\"_blank\" > WATCH: PFT Live with Mike Florio Watch PFT Live with Mike Florio and Chris Simms for the latest news coming out of the NFL every Monday to Friday at 7 a.m. ET on Peacock and the NBC Sports NOW channel. PFT Top Stories Patrick Mahomes on his injured elbow: Obviously, it felt good enough Myles Simmons , Myles Simmons , Dolphins waive Zane Gonzalez after three missed kicks on Sunday Josh Alper , Josh Alper , Panthers on pace to set new NFL records for most penalties and penalty yards this season Michael David Smith , Michael David Smith , ACC announces punishments after Louisville-North Carolina brawl Mike Florio , Mike Florio , Geno Smith getting tests on ankle, will start in Week 11 if healthy Josh Alper , Josh Alper , Report: Aidan Hutchinson is out for the season with torn ACL, likely additional damage Michael David Smith , Michael David Smith , Close Ad",
